  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>placement group</b> option is correct because EC2 placement groups permit instances to be launched physically close to each other inside the same Availability Zone, reducing network latency and maximizing network throughput—which is critical for high-performance, network-heavy workloads. Only when instances reside inside the same placement group can the full 10Gbps (or higher, depending on instance type) network capability be achieved. <br/><br/>The other options are incorrect for the following reasons:</p><ul><li><i>Opt for Provisioned IOPS (PIOPS) storage and mount multiple volumes to aggregate disk throughput.</i> This relates to disk I/O and storage performance, not network throughput, so it does not address the bottleneck described in the question.</li><li><i>Activate biplex networking on your servers for non-blocking, bidirectional traffic without switching latency.</i> 'Biplex networking' is not an AWS-supported term or feature—this is a distractor.</li><li><i>Distribute the instances into different VPCs to avoid saturating a single VPC’s Internet Gateway.</i> Placing instances in different VPCs will increase the latency and disrupt the high-throughput, low-latency network connections required for this workload. Internet Gateway bottlenecks refer to traffic leaving the VPC, not to high-speed networking between EC2 instances.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> For maximum network bandwidth and lowest latency between EC2 instances, always use placement groups—specifically 'cluster' placement groups for tightly coupled, high-performance workloads.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Which action is necessary to achieve gigabit-level network throughput on EC2 when you are already using cluster-compute 10Gbps instances with enhanced networking, and your workload is bottlenecked by the network, but you are not seeing expected 10Gbps performance?</p>",
